# /etc/default/pi-say -- what this machine's speaker needs.
#
# pi5-deck, wired: a Unitek Y-247A USB audio adapter (0d8c:0014), pinned to
# CARD=Spk by /etc/modprobe.d/snd-usb-audio-ids.conf (see
# etc/snd-usb-audio-ids.modprobe).
#
# Card numbers move with the order things are plugged in, and the product name
# is NOT the refuge it looks like: ALSA derives the id from the last word of
# the product string, so "USB Audio Device" and "USB PnP Sound Device" collide
# and whichever enumerates first takes the bare name while the other gets a
# "_1" suffix. On 2026-08-28 this deck saw it both ways within an hour -- once
# with pi-hear recording the speaker adapter's empty mic jack, once with pi-say
# playing into the microphone. Neither looks like a fault: the card is healthy,
# the format is right, the level is simply zero.
#
# So the ids are pinned by vid:pid and the names here are stable by
# construction. The previous adapter was a UGREEN (KTMicro, 12d1:0010), which
# named itself CARD=Audio; if it comes back, give it the Spk slot rather than
# editing every consumer.
PI_SAY_DEVICE=plughw:CARD=Spk,DEV=0

# The three settings below this line came from Bluetooth. Two of them are back,
# for a different reason -- the Unitek's hardware rates, not a sink's fixed
# ones -- and LEAD_MS stays gone:
#
#   PI_SAY_RATE / PI_SAY_CHANNELS  an A2DP sink runs at a fixed 48 kHz stereo,
#       and letting ALSA convert piper's 22050 Hz mono on the fly buzzed
#       audibly, so the cache had to hold pre-converted audio. The UGREEN took
#       22050 mono directly and needed neither. The Unitek offers only 48000
#       and 44100, stereo, so both are set again -- sox converts once into the
#       cache rather than ALSA converting on every playback. Read the rates
#       from /proc/asound/card*/stream0 before assuming a wired DAC is free.
#
#   PI_SAY_LEAD_MS  a Bluetooth speaker lets its amp sleep between streams and
#       took about a second to come back, eating the start of every phrase --
#       which on a confirmation is the half that says what was understood. 1800
#       ms of silence was padded into every cached file to cover it. Wired, the
#       first syllable is there, and every reply is 1.8 s earlier.
